EXT. DRIVE-IN MOVIE SCREEN - ROOFTOP SCAFFOLD - NIGHT

A vast white screen stands over a lot of weeds and abandoned speaker posts. At the top, a narrow catwalk of weathered planks runs behind the canvas. A few stars show through the clouds.

LEANDER PRZYBYLSKI (15, thin, hands in the pockets of a too-big hoodie) climbs over the last rung and sits at the edge with his legs hanging over nothing. Behind him, his sister WINIFRED (17, denim jacket, keys on a lanyard, jaw clenched) hauls herself up the ladder, breathing hard, and stands on the catwalk.

She finds him. She does not hurry.

WINIFRED
You scared me.

LEANDER
You came up anyway.

WINIFRED
Mom's looking everywhere.

LEANDER
Not here.

WINIFRED
She will be.

He doesn't turn. She walks along the planks, one hand trailing the canvas, which breathes like a sail. The wind carries her voice into the dark lot.

WINIFRED (CONT'D)
Dad's car is still in the driveway.

LEANDER
I know.

WINIFRED
He's not going to leave tonight.

LEANDER
He always says that.

WINIFRED
He does.

LEANDER
Then he does.

She sits beside him, close but not touching. Below, a cat crosses the lot and vanishes.

WINIFRED
Remember when we used to come here?

LEANDER
I remember the fries.

WINIFRED
Dad used to put his arm over the seat.

LEANDER
He used to put his arm over Mom.

WINIFRED
I was five.

LEANDER
I was three. I remember.

WINIFRED
You do not.

LEANDER
I remember everything.

That's true, and she knows it. She lowers her eyes.

WINIFRED
Come home.

LEANDER
Why?

WINIFRED
Because it's cold.

LEANDER
That's not a reason.

WINIFRED
Because I'm cold.

LEANDER
That's closer.

WINIFRED
Because I need you.

He turns at last. Looks at her. In the dark, the white screen glows faintly behind his head like a halo.

LEANDER
You never say that.

WINIFRED
I'm saying it.

LEANDER
Why now?

WINIFRED
Because he's going to leave tomorrow.

The words fall into the silence. Leander flinches, hard, like she struck him. He stands abruptly. The planks creak. She grabs his sleeve and he rips free.

LEANDER
You knew.

WINIFRED
Since Tuesday.

LEANDER
You knew since Tuesday and you didn't say.

WINIFRED
I was protecting you.

LEANDER
From what?

WINIFRED
From this.

LEANDER
This is worse!

His voice cracks and breaks over the empty lot. He kicks a loose plank. It tumbles off the edge and bounces down the scaffold with a long, cheap clatter. They both listen until it stops.

WINIFRED
Leander.

LEANDER
Don't.

WINIFRED
Look at me.

He doesn't. She steps in front of him, forcing it.

WINIFRED (CONT'D)
I'm sorry. I should have told you.

He crumples. She catches him before he reaches the planks. His shoulders shake. She holds him, her chin on his head, and over her shoulder the huge dark screen waits for a picture that will never come.
